{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793553223,"nonce":"aulyomdVEvZnB0ri2nrLskXu/PA=","message":"Design decision (rayde-00 with the user): write-back is built in two layers, snapshot publish first, then text updates.\n\nLayer 1, snapshot publish. Credential: the Worker mints a short-lived publish capability bound to workspace, owner and the restored generation when it opens a synced workspace, and hands it to the session. The session scans its own tree and publishes with it. Trigger: an explicit Save to cloud in the Cloud Sync panel. Conflict: a publish against a stale base fails the compare-and-swap and is shown as a conflict; nothing is overwritten. A restored tree has no .git, so this needs a scan that does not use git: walk the tree, apply the same exclude rules, carry the previous manifest's git section over.\n\nLayer 2, text updates through the session's Yrs documents. Today a session document starts with a random client id and takes the file's text as a \"disk\" edit (native/session/src/docs.rs), so two sides that load the same file independently have no shared history and their updates would duplicate text. Needs: a shared lineage (cloud-held Yrs state per file, or a deterministic base with a fixed client id as checkpoint.rs does), an update log per file in the workspace's Durable Object with compaction, and the desktop pulling updates and applying them to disk with the apply_diff rules. Binary files and create/delete/rename stay with the snapshot layer.","files":null}]}